The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471/) says one date-parsing fix will
cover the ticket. The evidence does not support treating every report that way.

<Callout kind="warn">
  Report counts come from the 2026-10-04 export, not the current queue.
  The engineering update below records the later closure. The linked GitHub issues track the split. Investigation tickets describe
  questions to resolve, not confirmed root causes.
</Callout>

<EngineeringUpdate />

## Report split

<ReportReview />

## Similar symptoms, different fixes

A shared parser can misread a correctly entered day-first date. Separately,
booking-v3 tells people to enter month first while interpreting their entry
in the tenant's day-first convention.

The parser fix addresses the first mechanism. The hint issue was not fixed:
engineering retired booking-v3 instead. These are different outcomes.
The remaining investigations need their own evidence before choosing a fix.

## What the automatic classifier says

<ScrollRegion label="Original automatic classification">
  <CauseTable ticket={4471} />
</ScrollRegion>

That table uses tenant locale, booking form, and channel. It does not read the
report body. Our review keeps its result visible but separates reports whose
descriptions do not establish the same date-swap mechanism.

Intake tags are browser-based guesses, not root-cause findings. Count unique
report IDs; do not count overlapping export rows again.

## Triage by evidence

The [accepted subject-matching rule](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) can merge
different problems and keep duplicates apart. The executable examples show why:

<DedupeExplorer />

Use tenant settings and the affected path to understand a report. Compare its
body and evidence before merging. Unknown context is a reason to investigate,
not to guess. Keep unresolved symptoms separate until a common cause is established.

This proposes a replacement for the [existing triage advice](/kb/triage/dedupe/).
Decision 0001 remains unchanged; a new decision record would be needed to replace it.

## Read the queue correctly

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports received and reports merged are different events. A spike in merging can
be backlog processing; it is not the start of the defect or a surge of new reports.

## Sources and next review

The counts above are computed from the exported reports and tenant directory.
The [original classifier](/evidence/causes.ts),
[desk notes](/evidence/desk-notes.json), and
[reviewed assignments](/evidence/report-review.json) explain the two views.

The parser and hint tickets can close with different reasons: fixed versus
not planned after form retirement. Do not call both outcomes a fix.
Gather the missing evidence listed in each investigation and revise its
assignment when it arrives. Reconcile newer desk data if available.
No report should disappear or be counted under more than one proposed ticket.

